=3D"color: #000000; font-size: inherit; font-weight: 400; line-height: inhe=
rit; text-decoration: inherit; font-family: arial; font-style: normal;">Bru=
ce, don't let collection accounts stress you out. Simply put, most debt col=
lectors are powerless! </span>They only have the power to scare you over th=
e telephone. You can stop their telephone calls and get them off of your cl=
ient=E2=80=99s credit reports with the proper letter, as I will show you in=
 <b style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0;" class=3D"">today=
=E2=80=99s lesson</b>. This breakdown of information is for you to understa=
nd the different ways debt collectors, junk debt buyers and=0Acollection at=
torneys can come at you or your clients and why the legal letter writing I =
will show you how to do will work in each situation. <br class=3D""><br cla=
ss=3D""> When a new collection hits the credit report or a debt collector b=
egins to contact a client over a debt, as the credit specialist you become =
their advisor. Your client may often seek advice from you in dealing with t=
he constant phone calls they are receiving or you may want to educate your =
client on what to do when faced with a call by a debt collector. <b style=
=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0;" class=3D"">Within this lesson</b=
> we will cover how to handle being contacted by a debt collector, consumer=
 rights, and <b style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0;" class=3D"">=
5 ways defeat debt</b> collectors and get collection accounts removed from =
the credit report. <br class=3D""></span><b style=3D"margin: 0; outline: no=
ne; padding: 0; font-size: 19px;" class=3D""><br class=3D""><div style=3D"m=
argin: 0;=0Aoutline: none; padding: 0; text-align: center;"><b class=3D"" s=
tyle=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; text-decoration: inherit;">No=
.1 </b></div>=0A</b><div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; fon=
t-size: 19px; text-align: center;"><b class=3D"" style=3D"margin: 0; outlin=
e: none; padding: 0; color: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;">Force The D=
ebt Collector to Communicate with You In Writing.</b></div>=0A<b style=3D"m=
argin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; font-size: 15px;" class=3D""> </b><br =
class=3D""><span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: 19px; font-weight: inh=
erit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D""> There is=
 No Need to Communicate with Debt Collectors over the Telephone. Debt colle=
ctors rely on your guilt about your debt and your inherent honesty to do th=
eir job FOR them. On the phone they know exactly what to say to push your b=
uttons and get you to admit you owe the debt. Their collection psychology i=
s to play on the guilt and fears of responsible debtors. Your collection ps=
ychology should be, "Be Difficult to Deal with!" <br class=3D""><br class=
=3D""> When educating your client=E2=80=99s about debt collection calls, it=
=E2=80=99s important to let them know that the best thing to do is not to a=
nswer the phone when a collector calls, or hang up when they identify thems=
elves. There is no need for telephone communications with anyone collecting=
 a debt. There is no=0Alegal substance to these telephone communications. <=
br class=3D""><br class=3D"">To pursue you legally, debt collectors must no=
tify you in writing. Therefore, You do not have to talk to them. I always w=
arn my clients that Debt Collectors will lie and say anything to scare them=
 into talking or making even the slightest admission of guilt and turning i=
t around in the collector's favor. If they must allow curiosity to get the =
better of them, advise your client to answer the phone, then immediately ge=
t the name, location and number of the collection firm calling. Next, tell =
them, "I do not discuss my personal financial affairs over the telephone wi=
th someone I do not know and I request that any further communication is do=
ne in writing." Then, advise them to immediately hang up the phone.<br clas=
s=3D""><br class=3D""></span><div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; paddin=
g: 0; text-align: left; font-size: 19px;"><b class=3D"" style=3D"margin: 0;=
 outline: none; padding: 0; color:=0Ainherit; text-decoration: underline;">=
Why These Instructions Are Important<br></b></div>=0A <div style=3D"margin:=
 0; outline: none; padding: 0; text-align: left; font-size: 19px;">The best=
 time to catch a debtor off guard and the hardest time for him or her to de=
ny and dispute the debt is on the first "ambush" call. This call usually ha=
ppens before you receive your mini-Miranda notice from this new collection =
agency telling you of your right to dispute the debt. The ambush call usual=
ly starts with the big lie, "I am calling to let you know that your court s=
ummons for [this debt] is going in the mail in a few days. This is your las=
t chance to pay the debt or set up a payment plan with us." Now they=
=E2=80=99ve got you scared. And, now they get you to admit to the debt and =
to share personal information, your SSI number, maybe even your bank accoun=
t number so they can quickly receive a partial payment from you to make the=
 phony lawsuit go away. <br></div>=0A<span style=3D"color: inherit; font-si=
ze: 19px; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inhe=
rit;" class=3D""><br class=3D""> This approach has frightened many consumer=
s who have experienced it. The most important thing to remember and advise =
your client do is deny and dispute. <br class=3D""> A supposed call from a =
"debt collector" is just a voice on the other end of the line, who could be=
 anyone. They have no legal standing. Your client=E2=80=99s personal financ=
es are none of their business. At this point, they have NOT received a mini=
-Miranda notice from the collection firm because if you remember from an ea=
rlier lesson, the collection agency is required to send notice within 5 day=
s of the initial contact and that notice is only valid when it is in writin=
g. This is why it is important to advise the client, when a debt collector =
calls, to tell them they do not discuss their personal finances over the ph=
one with unknown strangers. Then, hang up. Do not give=0Athem any informati=
on. Always force the collector to communicate in writing.<br class=3D""></s=
pan><b style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; font-size: 21px;" cla=
ss=3D""><br class=3D""><div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; =
text-align: center;"><b class=3D"" style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; paddi=
ng: 0; text-decoration: inherit;">No.2</b></div>=0A</b><div style=3D"margin=
: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; font-size: 21px; text-align: center;"><b cl=
ass=3D"" style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; color: inherit; tex=
t-decoration: inherit;">Debt Validation Letters </b></div>=0A<b style=3D"ma=
rgin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; font-size: 15px;" class=3D""> </b><br c=
lass=3D""><span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: 19px; font-weight: inhe=
rit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D""> The debt =
validation letter is the strongest weapon you have against debt collectors,=
 if you use it properly. <br><br class=3D""> Fair Debt Collection Practices=
 Act, Section 809(b), Validation of Debts (b) If the consumer notifies the =
debt collector in writing within the thirty-day period described in subsect=
ion (a) that the debt, or any portion thereof, is disputed, or that the con=
sumer requests the name and address of the original creditor, the debt coll=
ector shall cease collection of the debt, or any disputed portion thereof, =
until the debt collector obtains verification of the debt or any copy of a =
judgment, or the name and address of the original creditor . . . <br class=
=3D""><br>The Fair Debt Collection Practices Act=E2=80=99s Validation of De=
bts applies=0Ato communications from all entities trying to collect a debt =
including collection agencies, collection attorneys and junk debt buyers. T=
he Federal Trade Commission has interpreted the FDCPA Validation of Debts s=
ection to mean debt collectors must provide some proof from the original cr=
editor as to the identity of the consumer and the amount allegedly owed. In=
 an earlier lesson you received a resource of a letter from the FTC communi=
cating this interpretation; you can view it again here. <br class=3D""><br>=
Most debt collectors stop communicating with a consumer after they receive =
a debt validation letter (DV) from that alleged debtor. If they do not vali=
date, then they cannot legally communicate with you. They also cannot put a=
 negative listing on the credit report. <br class=3D""><br><span style=3D"c=
olor: inherit; font-size: inherit; font-weight: bold; line-height: inherit;=
 text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">To get the most out of the debt vali=
dation process:</span><br=0Aclass=3D"" style=3D"font-weight: bold;"><br>1) =
Send debt validation letters Certified Mail Return Receipt (CMRR). To cut c=
ost, you can opt to have the receipt delivered electronically by using the =
tracking number to log in and see when the letter was received.<br><br clas=
s=3D""> 2) Always reply to the first mini-Miranda from a new collector or j=
unk debt buyer within 30 days of receipt of it. <br><br class=3D""> 3) Alwa=
ys file the mini-Miranda and the DV response to maintain good records of yo=
ur contacts with debt collectors. <br><br class=3D""> 4) Always check the c=
lient=E2=80=99s state=E2=80=99s database of licensed debt collectors. If a =
collector is not on it, point out their violation in your DV letter.<br><br=
 class=3D""> 5) Always reply with another DV letter to any attempt to valid=
ate the debt stating that the attempt is insufficient. (A sample follow-up =
letter will be included in an upcoming lesson.) <br><br class=3D""> 6) Be s=
ure to tell the collector their claim is denied and=0Adisputed and to cease=
 all collection activities including putting negative listings on your cred=
it reports. <br><br class=3D""> 7) Demand a lot of information in your DV l=
etter (see letter below). Another goal of debt validation is to see what th=
e collector has for documentation on your debt. <br><br class=3D""> 8) Alwa=
ys demand complete documentation; your contract with them and a full accoun=
ting of all the payments, charges, interest and penalties that go into the =
total alleged amount owed, a sworn affidavit supporting that documentation,=
 as well as proof of ownership of the debt if it is a junk debt buyer comin=
g after you. <br><br class=3D""> What follows is a letter I have used to gr=
eat success. I have used red ink to explain why each component in the lette=
r is important.</span><br class=3D""></div>=0A</div>=0A </div>=0A<!--[if (g=
